# MIPS EXT / DEXTM / DEXTU: a guest instruction word drives a NEGATIVE shift count. Design the guards.

VERDICT line first, then the liveness token `CODEX_MIPSEXT_20260910`.

**Please do not simply ratify — check the premises, not just the derivations.** A recent panel here had
seven of nine seats confirm a premise this brief's author stated wrongly, and on THIS row a design review
already corrected my title: I had blamed plain `dext`, and **plain `DEXT` is clean** — the bad field pairs
reach the shared handler through the two variants' adjustments. So the premises below have already been
wrong once.

Ordinary bounds-correctness engineering on an instruction decoder in an emulator written in C99. The tree
is `C:\DocumentNoSnc\CC\GXEMUL\GXEMUL-SEC\src\cpus\cpu_mips_instr.c`; three unrelated 2021-vintage copies
of `src/` sit elsewhere under the project root and will give you line numbers off by hundreds.

## THE TWO EXECUTE HANDLERS, VERBATIM AND COMPLETE

```c
/*  ext:  Extract bitfield.   arg[2] = (msbd << 5) + lsb  */
X(ext)
{
	int msbd = ic->arg[2] >> 5, lsb = ic->arg[2] & 0x1f;
	int size = msbd + 1;
	uint32_t rs = reg(ic->arg[1]);
	uint32_t x = (rs << (32-lsb-size)) >> (32-lsb-size);
	reg(ic->arg[0]) = (int32_t) (x >> lsb);
}

/*  dext:  Extract bitfield (64-bit).   arg[2] = (msbd << 6) + lsb  */
X(dext)
{
	int msbd = ic->arg[2] >> 6, lsb = ic->arg[2] & 0x3f;
	int size = msbd + 1;
	uint64_t rs = reg(ic->arg[1]);
	uint64_t x = (rs << (uint64_t)(64-lsb-size)) >> (uint64_t)(64-lsb-size);
	reg(ic->arg[0]) = x >> lsb;
}
```

## THE DECODER, VERBATIM — and note the THREE opcodes share ONE block

```c
		case SPECIAL3_EXT:
			{
				int msbd = rd, lsb = (iword >> 6) & 0x1f;
				ic->arg[0] = (size_t)&cpu->cd.mips.gpr[rt];
				ic->arg[1] = (size_t)&cpu->cd.mips.gpr[rs];
				ic->arg[2] = (msbd << 5) + lsb;
				ic->f = instr(ext);
				if (rt == MIPS_GPR_ZERO)
					ic->f = instr(nop);
			}
			break;

		case SPECIAL3_DEXT:
		case SPECIAL3_DEXTM:
		case SPECIAL3_DEXTU:
			{
				int msbd = rd, lsb = (iword >> 6) & 0x1f;
				if (s6 == SPECIAL3_DEXTM)
					msbd += 32;
				if (s6 == SPECIAL3_DEXTU)
					lsb += 32;
				ic->arg[0] = (size_t)&cpu->cd.mips.gpr[rt];
				ic->arg[1] = (size_t)&cpu->cd.mips.gpr[rs];
				ic->arg[2] = (msbd << 6) + lsb;
				ic->f = instr(dext);
				if (rt == MIPS_GPR_ZERO)
					ic->f = instr(nop);
			}
			break;
```

An `ic->f = instr(reserved);` arm exists a few lines above this switch and is the file's own idiom for an
encoding the decoder refuses.

## MY DERIVATION, WHICH I WANT CHECKED RATHER THAN CONFIRMED

`rd` and the `lsb` field come straight out of a guest instruction word. `32-lsb-size` and `64-lsb-size` are
`int` expressions; a **negative** shift count is undefined behaviour, and on the usual hardware it masks to
the low bits of the count and produces a silently wrong extraction rather than a trap.

I count **496 of 1024 field pairs per opcode**, three times over:

| opcode | pos | size | in-spec constraint | pairs violating it |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `EXT`   | `lsb` 0..31        | `msbd+1` 1..32  | `0 < pos+size <= 32`  | 496 |
| `DEXT`  | `lsb` 0..31        | `msbd+1` 1..32  | `0 < pos+size <= 32`  | **0 — CLEAN** |
| `DEXTM` | `lsb` 0..31        | `msbd+33` 33..64 | `32 < pos+size <= 64` | 496 |
| `DEXTU` | `lsb+32` 32..63    | `msbd+1` 1..32  | `32 < pos+size <= 64` | 496 |

**Questions:**

1. **Are those four constraint rows what the MIPS64 architecture actually says?** In particular: is
   `DEXTM`'s size `msbd + 33` (which is what `msbd += 32` then `size = msbd + 1` computes), and is
   `DEXTU`'s pos `lsb + 32`? If the field adjustments are right, say so — I want to know whether the
   decoder's arithmetic is correct and only the RANGE is unchecked, or whether the adjustments are wrong
   too.
2. **Is `DEXT` genuinely clean?** My argument is that `lsb <= 31` and `size <= 32` give `64-lsb-size >= 1`
   always. Check it, because the row's title was already wrong once in the other direction.
3. Reproduce or refute the **496** figure for each of the three. Show the counting, do not assert it.
4. What does the architecture say these out-of-range encodings ARE — UNPREDICTABLE, reserved, or
   something with a defined result? Quote it. `_scratchpad/` holds `mips64_volII.txt`, `mips32_volII.txt`,
   `mips-iv.txt` and `mips_r4000.txt`; **cite only what you have read in those files.**

## WHAT I WANT DESIGNED

5. **The guards. Where do they go and what do they do?** My reading is that they go in the DECODER, not
   the handler, and that **placement is load-bearing**: the DEXT/DEXTM/DEXTU guard must sit AFTER the two
   `+= 32` adjustments, where the raw fields have become the real `pos` and `size`, and where the shared
   block covers all three at once with `DEXT` never able to trip it. **Is that right?** Write the exact
   guard text for both blocks.
6. **What DISPOSITION?** This project keeps an explicit option set for a refusal decision — DELETE,
   DECLINE SILENTLY, DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE, ACCEPT, FAULT (deliver the architectural exception),
   BACKED ACCEPT, IMPLEMENT. **Grep this file for what it already does with an invalid encoding**, and
   name the member with the tree's own precedent, not with an argument. Is `instr(reserved)` the right
   answer, and what does it do to the guest?
7. **Is the handler ALSO owed a defence?** A guard in the decoder protects the ordinary path. Is there any
   route to `X(dext)` or `X(ext)` that does not pass through this decode block?
8. **What is the smallest edit to your guard that keeps it compiling and lets a bad pair through?** Name
   the encoding and the wrong value it produces, so I can build that mutant. And: **could a guard placed
   BEFORE the `+= 32` adjustments look correct and be wrong?** If so, that is my ordering mutant.
9. **One round or three?** This project's rule: a round takes one site; a second joins only if it is in the
   same file, the same semantic class so **one fix design covers both**, and is covered by the **same
   detector with no new oracle**. `EXT` is 32-bit and its own handler; `DEXTM`/`DEXTU` share a block and a
   handler. Rule on it with the reason.
10. **Is there a WITNESS?** This project will not fix what it cannot reproduce. Can a real guest
    instruction reach this on an unmodified in-tree MIPS machine — is `EXT` in the base ISA of a machine
    this tree boots, or MIPS32r2-and-later only? Name the machine and the `-E` selector if one exists, and
    say plainly if none does.

